---
import { person } from '../data/site.ts';
---

<section class="band" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title" tabindex="-1">
  <div class="frame">
  <div class="col letter">
    <h2 id="about-title" class="sr-only">How I work</h2>
    <p data-reveal>
      I study computer science with a specialisation in cyber security at {person.university}, a university under India’s
      Ministry of Home Affairs. Most of what I build reads hostile input and decides what it is: a forwarded link, a prompt
      aimed at a model, a line from a firewall log.
    </p>
    <p data-reveal style="--i: 1">
      The lesson I keep relearning is that a good number can be wrong. My first LinkLens model looked excellent until a
      newsletter tag turned 63 honest links into scams. On ULPF, a flat 70,000 at every speed turned out to be a buffer, not
      a ceiling. So I write down where each figure came from and where it breaks, and I publish that next to the figure.
    </p>
    <p data-reveal style="--i: 2">
      I also write results for the person who received the link, not only for an analyst: what the page wanted, who runs it,
      and what to do now, in plain words. That is why a LinkLens report points to India’s 1930 cyber fraud helpline.
    </p>
    <p data-reveal style="--i: 3">
      I finish my B.Tech in July 2027. I am looking for work in detection, application security or security research.
    </p>
    <p class="sign" data-reveal style="--i: 4">Rohit</p>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .band {
    margin-top: var(--section-gap);
    padding-block: clamp(88px, 10vw, 152px);
    background: var(--field);
  }

  .letter {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.1em;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .sign {
    margin-top: 0.4em;
    font-size: var(--step-lead);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
</style>
